    "ls|LS17_S8_U_116": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 116 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.02.2022 \n \nVISTADOME COACHES \n \n*116. SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA:  \n \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to extend Vistadome \n Coaches on the Visakhapatnam -Araku r oute to Jeypore \n town of Koraput district in Odisha which is a prime tourist \n destination; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether any additional Vistadome Coaches have b een \n provided on any other tourist circuit route in the country, \n especially Odisha; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (d):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 116 BY SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 09.02.2022 REGARDING \nVISTADOME COACHES \n \n(a) and (b) No, Sir.  Vistadome coaches are attached to trains \nbased on passenger demand, operational feasibility and avail ability \nof coaches.  Presently, LHB Vistadome coaches have been attached \nto train no . 18551 (Visakhapatnam -Kirandul)/18552 (Kirandul -\nVisakhapatnam) Mail/Express train running on Visakhapatnam -\nKirandul section of East Coast Railway to run from Visakhapatnam \nto Arakku only.   Extension of these coaches upto Jeypore is having \noperational constraint at present.  \n \n(c) and (d) Yes, Sir.  Presently, 23 Vistadome coaches (Broad Gauge) \nhave been provided on Indian Railways.  Out of which, 4 vistadome \ncoaches have been allotted to Odisha. The vistadome coaches are \nallotted as follows: \nRailway Section No. of \nVistadome \ncoaches \nWestern Railway Ahmedabad- Kewadiya 2 \nCentral Railway Mumbai- Madgaon, Mumbai -Pune, \nDadar- Madgaon \n5 \nEast Coast \nRailway \nVisakhapatnam-Arakku 4 \nSouth Western \nRailway \nYashwantpur-Karwar, \nYashwantpur-Mangaluru \n6 \nNortheast \nFrontier Railway \nNew Jalpaiguri-Alipurduar, \nGuwahati-New Halflong \n5 \nNorthern Railway Kashmir Valley 1 \n  Total 23 \n*****                  ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_117": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 117 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nMINES PRODUCTION \n \n117.  SHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether  India‘s mines production has improved as compared to earlier years \namong other countries; and  \n \n(b)if so, the  details and present status thereof and the further effective action taken \nin this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF MINES, COAL AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n \n \n(a) & (b): As per the returns submitted under Mineral Conservation and Development \nRules (MCDR) 2017, t he production of few minerals viz. Garnet, Kyanite, Lead & \nZinc ore, Lead Concentrate, Marl, Phosphorite , Rock salt, Siliceous earth, Silver, Tin \nConcentrate and Zinc Concentrate has improved during the year 2020 -21 as \ncompared to the previous year. The mineral wise production of minerals under \nMCDR 2017 during 2018-19 to 2020-21 is at Annexure-I.  \n \nIndia is  among the top producers in the world of key minerals like bauxite, \nchromite, iron ore and manganese ore. The production of these minerals in the \nrespective top ten countries during the period 2015 -2019, as given in World Mineral \nProduction 2015-2019of British Geological Survey, along with growth, contribution to \nworld total (2019) and rank (in 2019) is at Annexure-II. The compound annual \ngrowth rate (CAGR) during 2015-2019 of iron ore production in India was the second \nhighest among the top ten producers. The CAGR of both chromium ore & \nconcentrates and manganese ore in India were higher than the global CAGR. \n \nThe Government has taken several policy reform measures to enhance \nproduction and increase the availability of minerals. The measures, inter-alia, include \nearly auction and  operationalization of expired mines; ease of doing business;  \nseamless transfer of all valid rights and approvals;  incentivizing for starting of mining \noperation and dispatch; transfer of mining leases; allowing captive mines to sell up to \n50% of the minerals produced; enhancing exploration activities, etc.  \n \n**** \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure-I referred to in reply to LS USQ No. 117 answered on 02.02.2022 \nregarding ‘MINES PRODUCTION’ \n \nProduction of minerals under MCDR, 2017 during 2018-19 to 2020-21 \nMineral Unit 2018-19 2019-20(P) 2020-21(P) \nBauxite t       2,36,89,619        2,18,23,793        2,03,68,665  \nChromite t          39,70,691           39,29,260           28,63,869  \nCopper Concentrate t            1,43,668             1,24,692             1,08,719  \nCopper Ore t          41,34,702           39,52,418           33,77,850  \nDiamond crt               38,437                28,816                13,917  \nFluorite (Graded) t                 1,079                  1,315                  1,052  \nGarnet t            1,23,404                     552                  9,307  \nGold Ore t            5,65,653             5,91,251             4,50,611  \nGold Primary kg                 1,664                  1,724                  1,126  \nGraphite t               39,030                31,991                30,168  \nIolite kg                      73                       90                       16  \nIron Ore tht            2,06,495             2,46,081             2,04,482  \nKyanite t                 4,889                  3,497                  4,925  \nLead & Zinc Ore t       1,37,52,295        1,44,79,032        1,54,55,343  \nLead Concentrate t            3,58,369             3,51,271             3,76,924  \nLimeshell t                 7,534                  4,600                        -    \nLimestone tht            3,79,975             3,59,331             3,49,170  \nMagnesite t            1,46,875                97,684                78,144  \nManganese Ore t          28,32,314           29,04,372           26,88,038  \nMarl t          18,90,308    