    "ls|LS17_S8_U_118": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.118 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.02.2022 \n \nRAILWAY PROJECTS IN KARNATAKA \n \n*118.  SHRI PRAJWAL REVANNA: \n  \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Railways proposes to undertake nine railway projects \nin Karnataka in association with the Department of Infrastructure \nof Government of Karnataka; \n \n(b) if so, the details of projects/tracks that are likely to be undertaken \nand the time by which these are likely to be completed;  \n \n(c) the details of the funds sanctioned and shared between Union and \nState Governments and the reasons for pendency of these lines \nsince long period of time; \n \n(d) whether the Union Government proposes to expedite Belur -\nChikmagalur and Belur-Hassan rail lines; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 118 BY SHRI PRAJWAL REVANNA TO BE ANSWERED IN \nLOK SABHA ON 09.02.2022 REGARDING RAILWAY PROJECTS IN \nKARNATAKA  \n \n(a) to (c): Railway projects are sanctioned and taken up Zonal Railway -\nwise an d not State -wise as the Indian Rail ways’ projects may span \nacross S tate boundaries. However, as on 01.04.2021, 35 Projects        \n(21 New Line and 14 Doubling), costing  `53,918 crore for 4,568 Km \nlength falling fully/partly in Karnataka are under different  stage of \nplanning/approval/execution, Out of which 1248 Km length has been \ncommissioned and an expenditure of  `15,537 crore has been incurred \nupto March 21. Out of these, 14 projects (12 New line and 02 doubling) \nhave been taken up on cost sharing basis with Government of \nKarnataka. Details of cost sharing project in Karnataka is appended.  \n \nRailway Projects in Karnataka are covered by South Western \nRailway (SWR), Central Railway (CR), Southern Railway (SR) and South \nCentral Railway (SCR). Railway Zone-wise details of all ongoing Railway \nprojects including cost, expenditure and outlay are made available in \npublic domain on Indian Railways website i.e.  \nwww.indianrailways.gov.in > Ministry of Railways >Railway Board \n>About Indian Railways >Railway Board Directorates >Finance (Budget) \n>Rail Budget/Pink Book (Year)> Railway wise Works Machinery and \nRolling Stock Programme. \n \nSince 2014, there is substantial increase in budget outlay for \ninfrastructure projects and safety works and commensurate \ncommissioning. Average annual Budget allocation for Infrastructure and \nsafety works, falling fully/ partly in State of  Karnataka,  during  2014-19  \n \n...2/- \n\n-2- \n \nhas been enhanced to `2,702 crore  per  year  from  `835 crore  per  year  \nduring  2009 -14,  which  is 224% more than average annual budget \noutlay of 2009 -14.  For financial year 2019 -20 budget allocation of \n`3,386 crore (306% more than average annual allocation of 2009 -14) and \nfor financial year 2020-21 `4,220 crore (405% more than average annual \nbudget allocation of 2009 -14) has been provided for these works. For \nfinancial year 2021 -22, highest ever budget outlay of `4,297 crore \n(original budget outlay  `4,204 crore and additional `93 crore) has been \nprovided for these works, which is 415% more than average annual \nbudget outlay of 2009-14.  \n \nDuring 2014 -21, 1,162 Km sections (281 Km of New line and 881 \nKm of Doubling) falling fully/partly in the State of Karnataka have been \ncommissioned at a n average rate 166 km per year, which is 47% more \nthan commissioning during 2009-14 (113 Km/Yr). \n \n The completion of any Railway project(s) depends on various \nfactors like deposition of cost share by cost sharing autho rity in cost \nsharing project(s),  quick land acquisition by State Government, forest \nclearance by officials of forest department, shifting of infringing \nutilities, statutory clearances from various authorities, geological and \ntopographical conditions of area, law and order situation in th e area of \nproject(s) site, number of working months in a year for particular project \nsite due to climatic conditions etc.  All these factors affect the \ncompletion time of the project(s). As such, confirmed completion time \nof project(s) cannot be ascertaine d at this stage. Nevertheless, every \neffort is being made to execute the project(s) expeditiously.  \n...3/- \n\n \n-3- \n \n(d) and (e): Kadur –Chikmagalur-Sakleshpur (93 Km) via Belur was \nincluded in Budget     1996 -97.  Subsequently Government of Karnataka \nhas agre ed to share the cost of the new line on a 50:50 basis from \nKadur-Chikmagalur and Chikmagalur –Sakleshpur on 50:50 and land free \nof cost. The section between Kadur –Chikmagalur (45 km) section was \ncommissioned during 2014. \n \n Government of Karnataka vide their letter dated 17.01.2019 has \nrequested to drop the section between Belur -Sakleshpur and proposed \nto sanction Belur -Hassan new line in lieu of it.  Government of \nKarnataka has also agreed for land free of cost and 50% const ruction \ncost of the proposed Belur – Hassan New line section. Accordingly, \nBelur – Hassan new line has been sanctioned.   \n Land acquisition and forestry clearance in Chikmangalur – Belur \nhas been taken up.  So far, 157.12 acre out of total required 324.36 acre \nland has been acquired and Stage – I approval of forestry clearance of \n2.67 acre land has been received and Stage -II approval has been taken \nup.   \n Final Location Survey (FLS) for preparation of Detailed Project \nReport (DPR) for Hassan – Belur section is in progress. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\nAPPENDIX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c ) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 118 BY SHRI PRAJWAL REVANNA TO BE ANSWERED IN \nLOK SABHA ON 09.02.2022 REGARDING RAILWAY PROJECTS IN \nKARNATAKA  \n (` in  crore) \nSN Name of project  Cost Sharing  Pattern  Cost \n (excluding land cost \nwhenever land is being \nprovided by Government \nof Karnataka at free of \ncost) \nA. New line projects \n1 Ginigera-Raichur (165 \nKm) part of \nMunirabad-\nMahabubnagar       \n(247 Km) \n50% cost by Govt. of \nKarnataka \n2565 \n2. Gadag-Wadi              \n(257 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project  \n2842 \n3. Bagalkot-Kudachi             \n(142 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n1530.09 \n4. Tumkur-Rayadurga   \n(207 Km) \n50% cost by Govt. of \nKarnataka and Govt. of \nAndra Pradesh in their \nportion \n1320 \n5. Tumkur- Davangere          \n(191 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n1676.85 \n6. Shimoga-Shikaripura- \nRanebennur (89 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n911.69 \n7. Kadur-Chikmagalur-Sakleshpur   (93 Km)  \n7a. Kadur – Chikmagalur \n(45 Km) \n50% cost by Govt. of \nKarnataka \n602 \n7b Chikmagalur – Belur \n(22.1 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n280.33 \n8. Hassan-Belur         \n(32 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n400 \n9. Shimoga-Harihar     \n(79 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n \n729.63 \n10. Belgaum-Dharwad \n(73 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n779.16 \n11. Whitefield-Kolar      \n(53 Km) \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n348.85 \n\n12. Bangalore (Hejjala) to \nChamarajanagar    \n(142 Km)  \nLand + 50% construction \ncost of the project \n1255 \nB. Doubling \n1. Yeshwantpur-\nChannasandra         \n(22 Km) (K-RIDE) \n50% cost by Govt. of \nKarnataka \n314.10 \n2. Baiyyappanahalli-\nHosur (48 Km)         \n(K-RIDE) \n50% cost by Govt. of \nKarnataka \n449 \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_119": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 119. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 2ND FEBRUARY, 2022. \n \nDOMESTIC AUTOMOTIVE INDUSTRY \n \n119.  SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                       ������������������������������ �������� \n(a)  whether the Covid pandemic, lockdown and  various restrictions along with a \nmultitude of headwinds, played spoilsport for the domestic automotive industry; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether any stimulus package announced for  the automotive industry to \nrecover from the worst position to come back to new normal; \n \n(d)  whether any concessions and incentives are  proposed to be offered to the \nindustry in view of the  slackening demand sentiment in the two -wheeler \ncategory; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \n������������������������������ �������������������������������������������� �����������������������\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI SOM PARKASH) \n \n(a) & (b):  Sir, the Automobile Industry has been affected in the last two years due \nto Covid -19 pandemic. The production and sales of Automobiles has \nalso been impacted due to the lock-down and other restrictions imposed \nto contain spread of Covid-19.  \n   \nThe sales of Automobiles has  recovered at a fast rate post the second \nwave of the pandemic and as per SIAM, the sales figures for April, 2021 \nto November, 2021 have shown an increase of 26%, 65%, and 36% over \nthe corresponding period of the previous year, in respect of Passenger \nvehicles, Commercial Vehicles and Three-wheelers, respectively.  \n  \n(c) to (e):   Government of India   has notified a Production Linked Incentive (PLI) \nScheme for the Automobile and Auto component sector   and a PLI \nScheme for Advanced Chemistry Cell (ACC) Battery Storage \nmanufacturing. FAME-II Scheme(Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of \nElectric Vehicles in India Phase II), which commenced on 1st April, 2019 \nfor three years, was also further extended for two years upto 31st March \n2024 covering electric buses, electric and hybrid four wheeler, electric \nthree wheeler and electric two wheelers. \n        \n*****  ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_12": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*12 \nFOR ANSWER ON 02/02/2022 \n \nDOMESTIC STEEL INDUSTRY \n \n*12. SHRI T.R. BAALU: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to  take more steps to strengthen domestic \nsteel industry and achieve sustainable operations in steel  industry through policy \nsupport, efficient resource  utilisation and research and development led production  \ninnovation; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the measures taken/proposed  to be taken by the \nGovernment in this regard; and \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STEEL     (SHRI RAM CHANDRA PRASAD SINGH) \n \n(a)to(c):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the Lok Sabha. \n \n******** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.*12 FOR ANSWER ON 02/02/2022 TABLED \nBY SHRI T.R. BAALU , MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING “ DOMESTIC \nSTEEL INDUSTRY” \n \n(a)to(c): Steel being a de -regulated sector, government acts as a facilitator, by \ncreating enabling environment for development of the sector.   National Steel Policy, \n2017 has mission to provide environment for attaining “self sufficiency” in steel \nproduction by providing policy support and guidance to steel producers. Towards this, \nthe action taken includes the following:- \n \n(i) Notification of Domestically Manufactured Iron & Steel Products (DMI&SP) \nPolicy for promoting procurement of Made in India steel.  \n \n(ii) Steel Scrap Recycling Policy has been  notified by Ministry of Steel which will \ncomplement the Motor Vehicles (Registration and Functions of Vehicle \nScrapping Facility) Rules, 2021 notified by Ministry of Road, Transport and \nHighways so as to enhance availability of scrap to facilitate sustain able steel \nmaking. \n \n(iii) Issuance of Steel Quality Control Order to stop manufacturing and import of \nnon-standardized steel.  \n \n(iv) Steel Import Monitoring System (SIMS) for advanced registration of steel \nimports.  \n \n(v) Production-Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme for Specialty Steel with an outlay of \n₹6,322 crores.  \n \n(vi) Engagement with steel producers including industry associations and leaders \nof domestic steel industry to address their issues by the concerned \nMinistries/Departments of the Central Government and State Governments.  \n \n(vii) Engagement with potential users; including from Railways, Defence, \nPetroleum and Natural Gas, Housing, Civil Aviation, Road Transport and \nHighways, Agriculture and Rural Development secto rs to enhance the steel \nusage and overall demand for steel in the country.  \n \n(viii) An eco -system of Research & Development has been created in the Steel \nSector, with the participation of all related stakeholders towards efficiency, \nsustainability and modernization. \n \n******** \n \n ",
