    "ls|LS17_S8_U_156": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.156 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 11TH FEBRUARY, 2022 \n \n \nSEATS FOR PG MEDICAL COURSES \n \n*156. SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a) the number of seats for Post Graduate(PG) medical courses which remained \nvacant in the last academic year, speciality-wise;  \n \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to ensure that PG medical seats are not left \nvacant in the future;  \n \n(c) whether the Government is planning to increase the number of medical seats for \nPost Graduate courses considering the shortage of specialized doctors;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 156* FOR 11TH FEBRUARY, 2022 \n \n(a) & (b) As informed by National Medical Commission (NMC), a total of 1,425 \nPostgraduate seats remained vacant in the last academic year 2020 -21 which \nincludes 1365 broad -specialty seats and 60 diploma seats. Specialty wise details \nare at Annexure-I.   \n \nFurther, a s per the provisions of the Regulations on Graduate Medical \nEducation and Post Graduate Medical Education, the Common Counseling for \nadmission to Central Medical Educational Institutions, All India Quota seats in \nGovernment Medical Colleges  and Deemed Universities is conducted by Medical \nCouncil Committee (MCC), Directorate General of Health Services, Ministry of \nHealth & Family Welfare, Government of India. Similarly, for admission to the \nState Quota seats in Government Medical College and all the seats in private \nmedical Colleges, the respective State Governments conduct common counseling \nand are responsible for filling up the seats.  The following steps are taken to ensure \nthat PG medical seats are not left vacant:- \n \n(i)  Reduction in the benchmark percentile for admission in P. G. degree & \ndiploma courses.  \n(ii) The counseling for all India quota PG (Broad Specialty) seats has been \nincreased to four rounds from the year 2021.  \n \n(c) to (e):    The Government has taken following  steps to further increase the \navailability of medical Post Graduate seats in the country: -  \n \ni. Centrally Sponsored Scheme for strengthening/ upgradation of existing State \nGovernment/Central Government Medical Colleges to increase PG seats.  \nii. Central Sector Scheme for upgradation of Government Medical Colleges by \nconstruction of Super Specialty Blocks. A total of 75 projects have been \napproved and 55 have been completed.  \niii. Under Central Sector Scheme for setting up of new AIIMS, 22 AIIMS have \nbeen approved. Undergraduate courses have started in 19 AIIMS.  \niv.  DNB qualification has been recognized for appointment as faculty to take \ncare of shortage of faculty.  \nv.   DNB diploma courses have been approved in 8 broad specialities.  \nvi. The ratio of te achers to students has been rationalized to increase the number \nof postgraduate medical seats in the country.  \nvii.  By amending the regulations, it has been made mandatory for all medical \ncolleges to start PG courses within 3 years from the date of their MBBS \nrecognition /continuation of recognition. \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure -I \n \nMD/ MS Courses (broad-specialty) \nS. No. Name of Course Total Vacant Seats in \nA.Y. 2020-21 \n1 MD - Social & Preventive Medicine / Community \nMedicine \n173 \n2 MD - Pharmacology 172 \n3 MD - Physiology 167 \n4 MD - Microbiology 158 \n5 MD/MS - Anatomy 151 \n6 MD - Bio-Chemistry 147 \n7 MD - Forensic Medicine/Forensic Medicine & \nToxicology \n72 \n8 MD - Pathology 64 \n9 MD - Anaesthesiology  45 \n10 MS - General Surgery 39 \n11 MD - General Medicine 25 \n12 MD - Radio Diagnosis/Radiology 24 \n13 MS - Orthopaedics 23 \n14 MD/MS - Ophthalmology 19 \n15 MD - Psychiatry 14 \n16 MD - Dermatology , Venereology & Leprosy 12 \n17 MD/MS - Obstetrics & Gynaecology 12 \n18 MD - Paediatrics 10 \n19 MD - Aviation Medicine/Aerospace Medicine 7 \n20 MS - ENT 7 \n21 MD - Tuberculosis & Respiratory Diseases / \nPulmonary Medicine \n6 \n22 MD - Radiotherapy/ Radiation Oncology 5 \n23 MD - Immuno Haematology & Blood \nTransfusion \n4 \n24 MD - Community Health Administration 2 \n25 MD - Hospital Administration 2 \n26 MD - Nuclear Medicine 2 \n27 MD - Family Medicine 1 \n28 MD - Palliative Medicine 1 \n29 MD - Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation 1 \nTotal MD/ MS Seats 1,365 \n \n  \n\n \n \n \nDiploma Courses \nS.No. Name of Course Total Vacant Seats in A.Y. 2020-21 \n1 Diploma in Health  Education 20 \n2 Diploma in Obstetrics & Gynaecology 9 \n3 Diploma in Ophthalmology 6 \n4 Diploma in Clinical Pathology 5 \n5 Diploma in Psychological Medicine  5 \n6 Diploma in Health Administration 3 \n7 Diploma in Radio-Diagnosis  3 \n8 Diploma in Anaesthesia 2 \n9 Diploma in Public Health 2 \n10 Diploma in Sports Medicine 2 \n11 Diploma in Oto-Rhino-Laryngology   1 \n12 Diploma in Dermatology 1 \n13 Diploma in Orthopaedics  1 \nTotal Diploma Seats 60 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_157": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO 157 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n  \nCoal Gasification \n  \n157.     SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA: \n             \n            Will the Minister of Coal be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government is in the process to achieve coal ga sification and \nliquefaction target of 100MT by 2030; \n(b) if so, whether this will include use of underground coal gasification technology as \nwell; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a):  Yes Sir, the Government has prepared a plan to achieve coal gasification and \nliquefaction of 100MT of coal by 2030. \n  \n(b) to (d):   No Sir. Underground Coal Gasification is not a  proven environmentally \nsustainable technology. Resultantly, Underground Coal Gasification (UCG) technology is not \nproven commercially across world. The pilot project at Australia was dropped due to \neconomic and environmental concerns. As such UCG is not u nder consideration for adoption \nin India. \n  \n***** \n \n ",
