    "ls|LS17_S8_U_49": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 49 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nSOLAR POWER PLANTS AT STATIONS \n \n49. SHRI Y. S. AVINASH REDDY: \n SHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHNA RAJU: \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to State: \n(a) whether the Government has been establishing solar power \nplants at all the railway stations in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the number of railway stations so far identified for installation of \nSolar Power Plants; particularly in the State of Andhra Pradesh; and \n(d)  the progress thereof as on date? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND ELECTRONICS & \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n(a) to (d) Indian Railways (IR) provides solar plants at Ra ilway stations \nbased on techno -economic feasibility and viability. IR has plans to \nprogressively provide solar power plants at more number of railway \nstations. So far, 1949 Railway stations across the country have been \nidentified for provision of rooftop s olar plants including 167 railway \nstations in Andhra Pradesh. Out of these identified Railway stations, \n1141 Railway stations have been provided with rooftop solar plants \nincluding 93 railway stations in the State of Andhra Pradesh. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_5": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 5 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nDOUBLING/ ELECTRIFICATION OF PUNE-MIRAJ-LONDHA RAILWAY LINE \n \n*5. SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any estimation of the total area of \nland that needs to be acquired in Satara district of Maharashtra \nfor the implementation of the Pune -Miraj-Londha railway line \ndoubling and electrification project;  \n \n(b) if so, the total land area to be acquired and the number of \nindividual farmers whose land is being or proposed to be acquired; \nand  \n \n(c) the total amount of compensation in rupees that has been already \npaid out and the amount that is likely to be paid out in the future \nto complete the total acquisition? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 5 BY SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 02.02.2022 REGARDING DOUBLING/ ELECTRIFICATION OF \nPUNE – MIRAJ – LONDHA RAILWAY LINE \n \n \n(a) to (c): Yes, Sir.  Pune - Miraj - Londa (466 km) doubling project has \nbeen sanctioned in 2015-16.  293 km falls in Maharashtra and 173 km in \nKarnataka. Anticipated cost of the project is `4,795 crore. An \nexpenditure of `1,345 crore has been incurred upto March, 2021 and an \noutlay of `555 crore has been provided for 2021-22.  \n \nSo far, total 126.35 km doubling including Pune – Shindwane \n(31.67 km ), Ambale – Rajwadi (4.72 km), Daundaj – Valha ( 8.52 km), \nShenoli – Kirloskarwadi (21.94 km), Kudachi – Ghataprabha (46.00 km) \nand Kirloskarwadi – Bhilwadi (13.5 km) has been completed. Work has \nbeen taken up in remaining section in available land. \n \nTotal area of land that needs to be acquired in Satara district of \nMaharashtra for the implementation of the Pune – Miraj – Londa \ndoubling and electrification pro ject has been worked out. In Satara \ndistrict of Maharashtra, total area of land to be acquired is 35.53 \nHectare and the number of individual farmers whose land is being or \nproposed to be acquired are approximately 4,378 numbers. \n \n Total amount of compensat ion paid till date is `25.09 crore for \n20.88 Hectare of land. The approximate amount of compensation to be \npaid for remaining land acquisition is `40.50 crore. \n \nContd…….2 \n \n\n- 2 - \n \nSince 2014 there has been substantial increase in Budget \nallocation for infrastructure projects and Safety works. Average Budget \nallocation for Infrastructure projects and safety works, falling fully/ \npartly in State of Maharashtra, during 2014 -19, has been enhanced to \n`4,801 crore per year  from `1,171 crore per year during 2009 -14. Thus, \nthe average budget allocation during 2014 -19 is 310% more  as \ncompared to average annual allocation during 2009 -14 ( `1,171 \ncrore/year). \n \nBudget allocation for these projects have been increased to \n`7,281 crore in 2019 -20 (522% more tha n average annual budget \nallocation of 2009 -14) and `6700 crore in 2020 -21 (472% more than the \naverage annual Budget allocation of 2009-14). \n \nFor Financial Year 2021 -22, highest ever Budget allocation of \n`8,547 crore {original Budget outlay (BE) `7,277 crore and additional \noutlay `1,270 crore} has been provided for these projects which is 630% \nmore than the average annual outlay of 2009-14 (`1,171 crore/year). \n \nDuring 2014 -21, 916 km sections (87 km of New Line, 136 km of \nGauge Conversion and 693 k m of Doubling) falling fully/ partly in State \nof Maharashtra have been commissioned at an average rate of 130.86 \nkm per year, which is 124% more than average annual commissioning \nduring 2009-14 (58.4 km/year). \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_50": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.50 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nGATIMAN EXPRESS \n \n†50. SHRI SHANKAR LALWANI: \n     \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any scheme to make \ntrain journey cheaper in AC Coaches;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d) whether the Railways has any proposal to run Gatiman \nExpress train between Indore -Delhi and Indore-Mumbai in the \nnear future; \n(e) if so, the time by when it is likely to be started; \n(f) if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(g) the scheme made by the Government to make the rail journey \nsafe and comfortable?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (g)  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House . \n \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (g) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 50 BY SHRI SHANKAR LA LWANI TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 02.02.2022 REGARDING GATIMAN \nEXPRESS \n \n(a) Yes, Sir.  \n(b) To make the train journey affordable by Air - conditioned (AC) class, \nGovernment has introduced AC -3 tier Economy coaches. The base \nfare for 3AC Economy coaches has been fixed lower than the \nexisting fare of Mail/Express AC 3 tier coaches.  \n(c) Does not arise. \n(d) to (f) At present, 01 pair of Gatimaan Express, namely, 12049/12050 \nVirangana Lakshmibai  – Hazrat Nizamuddin Gatimaan Express is \noperational on Indian Railways. Further, at present, there is no \nimmediate proposal to introduce Gatimaan Express on Indore -Delhi \nand Indore -Mumbai sector.  However, introduction of trains is an \nongoing process over Indian Railways, subject to operational \nfeasibility and commercial justification. \n (g)   It is a continual endeavour of Indian Railways (IR) to make rail \njourney safe r and more comfortable for passengers. Some of the \nsteps in this regard are listed below:- \ni. State-of-the-art Train -set Vande Bharat services have been \nintroduced between New Delhi – Varanasi and New Delhi- Shri \nMata Vaishno Devi Katra. These trains have ult ra modern \nfeatures like quick acceleration, on board infotainment and \n\nGlobal Positioning System (GPS) based passenger information \nsystem, automatic sliding doors, retractable footsteps, Zero \ndischarge vacuum bio toilets etc. \nii. Various premium train services like Humsafar, Tejas, \nAntyodaya, Utkrisht Double Decker Air -conditioned Yatri \n(UDAY), Mahamana and coaches like Deen Dayalu and \nAnubhuti, which have upgraded interiors / exteriors and \nimproved passenger amenities, have been introduced in \nservice in various train services over IR.  \niii. IR has also introduced State -of-the-art Vistadome coaches on \nLinke Hofmann Busch (LHB) platform in various trains over IR. \nThese coaches have several modern features and amenities \nproviding panoramic view, through wider body side windows \nas well as through transparent sections in the roof, thus \nenabling the passengers to enjoy the scenic beauty of the \nplaces through which they travel. \niv. IR has decided to proliferate LHB coaches, which are \ntechnologically superior and have better ridi ng, aesthetics \nand safety features than conventional Integral Coach Factory \n(ICF) type coaches. Production Units of IR have stopped the \nmanufacturing of ICF type coaches and are manufacturing \nonly LHB coaches from the year 2018-19 onwards. \n\nv. IR has also upgr aded rakes of various Mail / Express trains \nrunning with ICF coaches under  Project Utkrisht . Under  \nProject Swarn, rakes of Rajdhani and Shatabdi trains have \nbeen upgraded across multiple dimensions, which include \ncoach interiors, toilets, onboard cleanline ss, staff behaviour, \nlinen, etc. \nvi. Smart coaches with ultra modern features like Smart Public \naddress and passenger information system, Smart HVAC \n(Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning system), Smart \nsecurity and surveillance system etc. have also been \nmanufactured. \nvii. A new variant of LHB AC three-tier coach- AC Economy coach, \nhas also been introduced in service with enhanced amenities \nand increased passenger carrying capacity. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_51": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 51 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nCoal Production by States  \n51. SHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR: \n             \nWill the Minister of Coal be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of the names of coal producing states along with the quantum of coal \nthey are producing annually; \n \n(b) whether any survey for accessing annual consumption of coal has been \nconducted by the Government; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) whether any action plan has been chalked out by the Government to meet the \nshortfall and to increase production of coal and if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS, COAL AND MINES  \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a): State-wise raw coal production in the country during 2020 -21 and 2021-22(till \nDecember) is given below: \nPRODUCTION DATA                 \n(Figure in Million tonnes) \nSTATE/Year 2020-21 \n2021-22 \nUpto Dec-21 \n(Provisional) \nASSAM 0.036 0.000 \nCHHATTISGARH 158.409 98.558 \nJAMMU & KASHMIR 0.012 0.009 \nJHARKHAND 119.296 79.957 \nMADHYA PRADESH 132.531 98.236 \nMAHARASTRA 47.435 32.971 \nODISHA 154.150 127.928 \nTELANGANA 52.603 48.171 \nUTTAR PRADESH 17.016 12.800 \nWEST BENGAL 34.596 23.715 \nAll India 716.084 522.345 \n        Source: CCO Provisional Coal Statistics 2021-21  \n \n(b) & (c):  As per initial study report of NITI Aayog, the estimated coal demand of \nvarious sectors viz.. Power generation, Iron &  Steel, Cement, Aluminium, and \nCaptive Power Production, is about 1192-1325 Million Tonnes (MT) in 2030. \n(d): The following action has been taken by Government to further enhance the \nproduction of the coal in the country: \n\ni. Commercial Auction of coal on reve nue share mechanism: Auction of \ncommercial mining on Revenue Sharing Mechanism was launched on \n18.06.2020 by Hon’ble Prime Minister. Under this scheme, total of 2 \ntranches have been successfully completed and third Tranche is currently \nunder process. From these two tranches total of 28 coal mines have been \nsuccessfully auctioned for which Vesting order have in signed for 27 coal \nmines.  \nii. Allow sale of excess coal production : The Ministry of Coal has amended \nMineral Concession Rules, 1960 with a view to allow ing sale of coal or \nlignite, on payment of additional amount, by the lessee of a captive mine up \nto 50 percent of the total coal or lignite produced in a financial year, after \nmeeting the requirement of the end use plant linked with the mine. Earlier \nthis year, the Mines and Minerals (Development & Regulation) Act had been \namended to this effect. This is applicable for both the private and public \nsector captive mines. With this amendment, the Government has paved the \nway for releasing of additional coal in the market by greater utilization of \nmining capacities of captive coal and lignite blocks,which were being only \npartly utilized owing to limited production of coal for meeting their captive \nneeds. \niii. Rolling auction: In order to expedite the process for conducting auction and \nto carryout more rounds of auction in a year, a mechanism of rolling \nauctions of coal mines has been planned. Under this mechanism, upon \ncompletion of the electronic auction process of a tranche, the next tranche of \nauction would be launched for following mines: \na. Mines where no bid or only single bid was received in the previous \ntranche of auction (except for those mines where Ministry of Coal decides \nto go for second attempt of auction) \nb .New mines, if any, identified by Ministry of Coal \nIn the current III tranche of commercial auction, total of 48 coal \nmines have been rolled over from the II tranche of mines. \niv. Single Window Clearance : The Union government has already launched \nSingle Window Clearance portal on 11.01.2021 for the coal sector to speed \nup the operationalisation of coal mines.  It is an unified platform that \nfacilitates grant of clearances and approvals required for starting a coal mine \nin India. Now, the complete process shall be facilitated through Single \nWindow Clearance Portal, which will map not only the relevant application \nformats, but also process flow for grant of approval or clearances. \nv. Coal India Ltd. has envisage d a coal production programme of one Billion \nTonne from CIL mines.   CIL has taken the following steps to achieve the \ntarget of augmentation of coal production capacity: \n1. 15 Projects identified with a Capacity of about 160 MTPA (Million Tonnes per \nAnnum) to be operated by Mine Developer cum Operator mode. \n2. Capacity addition through special dispensation in Environment Clearance \nunder clause 7(ii) of Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) 2006 \n3. CIL has taken steps to upgrade the mechanized coal transportation and \nloading system under 'First Mile Connectivity' projects.  \n  \n**** \n  ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_52": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.52 \nFOR ANSWER ON 02/02/2022 \n \nPOLICIES FOR DOMESTIC STEEL SECTOR \n \n52. SHRI KHAGEN MURMU: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated policies  aimed at bolstering the \ndomestic steel sector; and \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STEEL     (SHRI RAM CHANDRA PRASAD SINGH) \n \n(a)&(b): Steel being a de-regulated sector, government acts as a facilitator, by \ncreating enabling environment for development of the sector.   National Steel Policy, \n2017 has mission to provide environment for attaining “self sufficiency” in steel \nproduction by providing policy  support and guidance to steel producers.   Towards \nthis, the action taken includes the following:- \n \n1. Notification of Domestic ally Manufactured lron & Steel P roducts (DMl&SP) \nPolicy for promoting procurement of Made in India steel.  \n2. Notification of Steel Sc rap Recycling Policy to enhance the availability of \ndomestically generated scrap.  \n3. Issuance of Steel Quality Control Order to stop manufacturing and import of \nnon-standardized steel.  \n4. Steel I mport Monitoring System (SIMS) for advanced registration of steel  \nimports.  \n5. Production Linked Incentive (PLl) Scheme for Specialty Steel with an outlay of \nRs 6,322 crores.  \n6. Engagement with various stakeholders including industry association and \nleaders of domestic steel industry to address their issues by the concerned \nMinistries/Departments of the Central Government and State Governments.  \n7. Engagement with relevant stakeholders including from Railways, Defence, \nPetroleum and Natural Gas, Housing, C ivil Aviation, Road Transport and \nHighways, Agriculture and Rural Developm ent sectors to enhance the steel \nusage and overall demand for steel in the country.  \n8. Establishment of Project Development Cell in the Ministry to attract and \nfacilitate investment in the steel sector. \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S8_U_53": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION  \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.53 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n  \nCONSUMER PRICE INDEX \n  \n53.   SHRI M.K. RAGHAVAN : \n  \nWill the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has any plans to ensure the \nrepresentation of Members of Parliament in supervision of the \nimplementation of Central and Central funded State projects i n \ntheir respective constituencies; \n(b)  if so, the details regarding the steps being taken for the same; \n(c) whether the Government has any data regarding the rising \nbenchmark of consumer price index of the country; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION , MINISTER OF \nSTATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS [RAO \nINDERJIT SINGH] \n(a) and (b) : The guidelines of the Members of Parliament Local Area \nDevelopment Scheme (MPLADS) administered by Ministry of Statistics \nand Programme Implementation (MOSPI) interalia provide that in the \nmeetings held by the District Authorities to review the MPLADS wo rks \nimplementation, they shall invite the Hon'ble Members of Parliament \nconcerned. \n\n(c): MOSPI releases Monthly All India Consumer Price Index (CPI) with \nBase 2012=100, and inflation rates based thereon, for the country. \n (d): The monthly inflation rates based on all India CPI for 2021 are given \nbelow:  \nGeneral Inflation based upon All India Consumer Price  \n(Base 2012 = 100) \nMonth All India \nJanuary, 2021 4.06 \nFebruary, 2021 5.03 \nMarch, 2021 5.52 \nApril, 2021 4.23 \nMay, 2021 6.30 \nJune, 2021 6.26 \nJuly, 2021 5.59 \nAugust, 2021 5.30 \nSeptember, 2021 4.35 \nOctober, 2021 4.48 \nNovember, 2021 4.91 \nDecember, 2021 5.59 (Provisional) \n \n******* ",
